\section{Two Ways to Specify a Schedule}{


\strong{Option 1 — Model-based (recommended)}

Supply \code{Pars}, a named list whose element names correspond to the arguments
of a built-in model function (see, e.g., \code{\link[=LengthModels]{LengthModels()}},
\code{\link[=MaturityModels]{MaturityModels()}}, \code{\link[=NaturalMortalityModels]{NaturalMortalityModels()}}). The model is then resolved
automatically by \code{\link[=FindModel]{FindModel()}}, which matches the parameter names in \code{Pars}
to the formal arguments of candidate model functions. You may also set
\code{Model} explicitly to a character string naming the model or to a custom R
function — see \emph{Custom Models} below.

When model-based, \code{Pars} are expanded to \verb{Sim × Year} arrays by
\code{\link[=Populate]{Populate()}}, the model function is called to produce \code{MeanAtAge} (or
\code{MeanAtLength}/\code{MeanAtWeight} for size-based models), and any existing
values in \verb{MeanAt*} slots are \strong{overwritten}.

\strong{Option 2 — Direct array}

Leave \code{Pars = list()} (the default) and supply one of \code{MeanAtAge},
\code{MeanAtLength}, or \code{MeanAtWeight} directly as a numeric array. No model is
inferred and no overwriting occurs.

These two options are mutually exclusive: if \code{Pars} contains valid,
non-\code{NA} named values that match a model, it takes precedence and any
values supplied in \verb{MeanAt*} will be replaced.
}

\section{Populating \code{Pars} — Input Formats}{


Each element of \code{Pars} may be supplied in any of the following forms. All
are converted internally to \verb{Sim × Year} (or \verb{Sim × Year × Area}) arrays
before the model function is called.

\strong{Scalar} — a single value applied to all simulations and years:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{Pars = list(M = 0.2)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Length-2 bounds vector} — values are sampled independently for each
simulation from \code{Uniform(lower, upper)}. When \code{nSim = 1}, the midpoint is
used:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{Pars = list(M = c(0.1, 0.3))   # U(0.1, 0.3) across simulations
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Length-\code{nSim} vector} — one value per simulation, constant across years:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{Pars = list(M = c(0.15, 0.20, 0.25, ...))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{\verb{Sim × Year} array with named dimensions} — full stochastic,
time-varying specification. Only the years where the value \emph{changes} need
to be included; \code{\link[=Extend]{Extend()}} fills intermediate and future years
automatically by forward-filling from the most recent supplied year:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{m_arr <- array(
  c(0.2, 0.4),              # two change points
  dim      = c(1, 2),
  dimnames = list(Sim = 1, Year = c(1990, 2010))
)
Pars = list(M = m_arr)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Temporal random walk} — append a partner entry named \verb{<ParName>SD} to
\code{Pars}. A mean-preserving log-normal random walk is then applied to the
base parameter across years, and the \code{SD} entry is removed from
\code{Pars} after use:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{Pars = list(M = 0.2, MSD = 0.1)   # M with log-normal inter-annual variation
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{\verb{MeanAt*} Array Formats and Dimension Conventions}{


All \verb{MeanAt*} arrays use named dimensions. The expected dimension names
depend on the array type:\tabular{ll}{
   Array \tab Dimensions \cr
   \code{MeanAtAge} \tab \verb{Sim × Age × Year} \cr
   \code{MeanAtLength} \tab \verb{Sim × Class × Year} \cr
   \code{MeanAtWeight} \tab \verb{Sim × Class × Year} \cr
}


As with \code{Pars}, you only need to supply the years at which the schedule
\emph{changes}. \code{\link[=Extend]{Extend()}} forward-fills all other years automatically.
Similarly, a single simulation (\code{Sim} dimension of length 1) is replicated
to all \code{nSim} simulations.

A numeric vector of length \code{nAge} is also accepted for \code{MeanAtAge} and is
promoted to a \verb{1 × nAge × 1} array automatically.
}

\section{How \code{MeanAtLength} and \code{MeanAtWeight} Relate to \code{MeanAtAge}}{


\code{openMSE} uses age-based accounting, so \code{MeanAtAge}
is ultimately what is needed. \code{MeanAtLength} and \code{MeanAtWeight} serve two
roles:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Generating length- or weight- data} (e.g., observed size
distributions, length-based data).
\item \strong{Conversion to \code{MeanAtAge}} when a schedule is naturally expressed on
a size axis (e.g., length-based maturity ogives, length-based
selectivity).
}

The conversion from size to age uses the Age-Length Key (\code{ALK}) stored in
the \link[=length-class]{length} object, or the Age-Weight Key (\code{AWK}) from the
\link[=weight-class]{weight} object:
\itemize{
\item If \code{MeanAtLength} is populated and \code{MeanAtAge} is not, \code{\link[=Populate]{Populate()}}
converts \code{MeanAtLength} → \code{MeanAtAge} via the \code{ALK}.
\item If \code{MeanAtWeight} is populated and \code{MeanAtAge} is not, \code{\link[=Populate]{Populate()}}
converts \code{MeanAtWeight} → \code{MeanAtAge} via the \code{AWK}.
\item If \code{MeanAtAge} is already populated it is \strong{not} overwritten by this
conversion.
\item If both \code{MeanAtLength} and \code{MeanAtAge} are populated, \code{MeanAtAge} takes
precedence and \code{MeanAtLength} is used only for size-indexed output.
}

For \strong{model-based} specification, whether the model populates \code{MeanAtAge}
directly or via \code{MeanAtLength} depends on the model's function signature:
models that accept a \code{Length} argument produce \code{MeanAtLength} first and then
convert to \code{MeanAtAge} via the \code{ALK}; age-based models populate \code{MeanAtAge}
directly.
}

\section{Model Resolution — \code{Pars}, \code{Model}, and \code{FindModel()}}{


When \code{Pars} is non-empty and \code{Model} is \code{NULL}, \code{\link[=FindModel]{FindModel()}} is called
automatically. It scans the candidate model functions for the object class
(e.g., all functions with class \code{"LengthModel"}) and finds the one whose
formal argument names match the names in \code{Pars} exactly (excluding
auxiliary arguments such as \code{Ages}, \code{Length}, \code{Weight}). SD-suffixed
entries are ignored during matching.

If no unique match is found, an informative error is thrown directing the
user to the relevant \verb{*Models()} function.

You may bypass automatic inference by setting \code{Model} explicitly:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Character string}: the name of a built-in model (must exist as an
exported function of the appropriate class).
\item \strong{R function}: a custom function whose formal arguments match the names
in \code{Pars}. Auxiliary arguments (\code{Ages}, \code{Length}, \code{Weight}, etc.) may
also be present.
}

If \code{object@Model} is already a function object, \code{\link[=FindModel]{FindModel()}} returns it
unchanged — no further matching is attempted.
}

\section{Custom Models}{


Any R function can be used as a model by passing it to \code{Model}. The
function must accept arguments whose names match the elements of \code{Pars},
plus any required auxiliary arguments (\code{Ages}, \code{Length}, \code{Weight}, etc.
as appropriate). It must return a named \verb{Sim × Age × Year} array
(or \verb{Sim × Class × Year} for size-based outputs):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{my_M <- function(M, Ages) \{
  # age-varying M: decline from M to M/2 over age classes
  m_vec <- seq(M, M / 2, length.out = length(Ages))
  array(m_vec, dim = c(1, length(Ages), 1),
        dimnames = list(Sim = 1, Age = Ages, Year = 1))
\}

NaturalMortality(Pars = list(M = 0.3), Model = my_M)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

